      "end": 52.85,
      "text": "An LLM cannot answer from private documents it never saw. RAG lets it retrieve evidence before answering. First, before anyone asks, documents are split into useful chunks. An embedding model converts each chunk into a vector, and a vector database stores the vector with its original text. A user asks, \u201cWhat is our refund policy?\u201d The same embedding model creates a query vector. Retrieval compares it with stored vectors and selects the most relevant chunks. The system does not train the language model again. It builds a prompt with instructions, the question, and retrieved evidence. The LLM reads that context and generates an answer grounded in those chunks, ideally with citations. If evidence is missing, it should say so. RAG can reduce unsupported answers, but does not guarantee truth. Poor chunks, weak retrieval, or stale documents can still fail. Remember the flow: index documents, retrieve evidence, augment the prompt, then generate.",
